  "Source": "P. W. Baumeister, \"Optical Tunneling and Its Applications to Optical Filters,\" Appl. Opt. 6, 897-904 (1967). doi:10.1364/AO.6.000897 - Table I column 'Fig. 3' (p. 899); text p. 898 ('optical density is greater than 2.0 when the thickness of the layer exceeds one wavelength'), Fig. 3 caption",
  "Notes": "Single tunnel layer (index 1.35) between two prisms at 60 deg - frustrated total reflection; the thickness sweep of Fig. 3 evaluated at a thickness of one wavelength\nSpecification: None (illustration of R and T vs tunnel-layer thickness, 0 to 1 wavelength, at 60 deg incidence).\nReported: Text p. 898: optical density > 2.0 (T < 0.01) when the layer thickness exceeds one wavelength, at 60 deg (both polarizations; s and p curves not resolved in Fig. 3). Fig. 3: R rises from 0 at zero thickness to ~1 near one wavelength, T falls correspondingly (curves only, not tabulated).\nNotes: Fig. 3 plots R and T vs physical thickness in wavelengths (0-1); the layer is non-dispersive so any wavelength works: here lambda = reference_nm = 1000 nm and thickness 1000 nm = 1.0 wavelength (the end point of the sweep, where the text quotes OD > 2). To reproduce the whole Fig. 3, sweep the thickness 0-1000 nm at 1000 nm. Media: Table I gives 1.72 for incident and emergent medium, but the text (p. 898) says 'a prism of index 1.70' - 1.72 (Table I) is used. Angle 60 deg is inside the 1.72 prism (beyond the critical angle 51.7 deg). Independent check (own TMM): T at d = 1 wave is 0.0013 (s) / 0.0014 (p) with n0 = 1.72, i.e. OD ~2.9.\nLayers from Table I column 'Fig. 3' (p. 899); text p. 898 ('optical density is greater than 2.0 when the thickness of the layer exceeds one wavelength'), Fig. 3 caption; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours Ts 1.3e-3, Tp 1.4e-3 at 60 deg; paper optical density over 2 (T < 0.01) for a layer a wavelength thick.",
